{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MWXSVDRuUoXZdW1jK7cC1H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SdZHiEHJYtcmeByPFEzNH4","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Provide an independent, blind review. Do NOT speculate about whether results are \"good\" or \"bad\" in any absolute sense — evaluate the paper on its own merits.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 9=strong accept, 8=clear accept, 7=accept, 6=marginally above, 5=marginally below, 4=rejection, 3=clear rejection, 2=strong rejection, 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values robot learning relevance, real-world applicability\n\n## Paper: \"Importance Margin Maximization for Robust VLA Token Compression: Navigating the Robustness-Efficiency Pareto Frontier\"\n\n### Core Idea\nVLA token compression is adversarially fragile (~33% preservation at ε=0.01). The paper traces this to vanishingly small importance margins Δk≈0.01, and proposes to maximize Δk via:\n1. Formal bound P(top-k flip) ≤ k·ε·L/Δk (used as design criterion, not runtime certificate)\n2. ACIS: lightweight 2-layer cross-attention importance scorer (2.8M params)\n3. Two-stage training: Stage 1 train ACIS (backbone frozen), Stage 2 LoRA fine-tune backbone (ACIS frozen)\n\n### Claims\n- NOT claiming lossless compression — explicitly frames as Pareto tradeoff\n- 7-13pp SR reduction for +52-55pp preservation improvement\n- Compression-agnostic: works across FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, ACIS\n- Generalizes across SmolVLA (2B) and OpenVLA (7B)\n- Dominates adversarial training, randomized smoothing, Jacobian regularization\n\n### Related Work as Presented\n**VLA Token Compression:** FastV [3], VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache [4], VLA-ADP, LightVLA, GridS — all focus on efficiency, none address adversarial robustness.\n\n**Adversarial Robustness of Token Compression:** \"Less Is More Until It Breaks\" [5] demonstrated fragility. CAGE [6] formalized certification but no training-time remedy. This paper claims to bridge the gap.\n\n**Robust VLA Models:** RobustVLA (Jacobian reg), ProGAL-VLA (adversarial training) — generic defenses, don't target compression-specific robustness. Paper shows these get only 48-52% preservation vs IMM's 88%.\n\nReferences: Only 7 references total in the paper.\n\n### Key Results Summary\n- LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation dims): 54.4% avg SR under 50% compression (vs 31.9% compress-only, 67.1% no-compress)\n- Preservation: 32% → 87% (+55pp)\n- Cross-model: works on 2B and 7B, but margin ratio drops from 93.2× to 12.5×\n- Cross-compression: +52pp preservation avg across 3 external methods, but G3 increases +0.16-0.22\n- Defense comparison: IMM dominates in preservation (88%) with lowest SR drop (-9.3pp) and overhead (1.35× train)\n- Formal bound: 3-19× loose, positioned as design criterion\n- Token analysis: pruned tokens NOT noise (attn weight ratio 0.69, p=0.08 not significant)\n- G3<1.0 only on aloha_sim_insertion, NOT on LIBERO (G3=1.12-1.25)\n- ACIS is hard dependency: bypass drops SR by 26pp\n\n### Important Caveats\n- Only 7 references — very sparse bibliography\n- All experiments in simulation only\n- The formal bound is explicitly not tight enough for certification\n- The \"Pareto\" framing is a retreat from stronger claims (lossless)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the core idea (margin maximization for compression robustness) genuinely novel? How does it relate to margin-based methods in adversarial ML more broadly? Is the contribution incremental or significant?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are important baselines missing? Is the positioning fair? Is the bibliography adequate?\n3. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately scoped? Is the \"Pareto\" narrative honest and convincing? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n4. **Significance for CoRL**: Is this relevant and impactful for the robot learning community?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: X/4\n- Related Work Coverage: X/4\n- Significance: X/4\n- Overall Score: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1113,"cache_read_input_tokens":67742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1113},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350","uuid":"df619d4c-2dfb-4485-9a32-eb14811624c8"}
