{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0133JwgvRJEhAiXysbpkGUHA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[conference]{corl_2026}\n2\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n3\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n4\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n9\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n10\t\n11\t\\usepackage{amsthm}\n12\t\\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}\n13\t\n14\t\\newcommand{\\ours}{IMM}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\acis}{ACIS}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\deltak}{\\Delta_k}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Importance Margin Maximization: A Robustness-Aware Framework\\\\for Vision-Language-Action Token Compression}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{\n21\t  Anonymous Authors\n22\t}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t%==============================================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t%==============================================================================\n30\tToken compression is essential for deploying Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models on resource-constrained robots, yet existing compression methods are brittle: small input perturbations destabilize importance rankings and cause safety-critical tokens to be discarded.\n31\tWe introduce \\emph{Importance Margin Maximization} (\\ours{}), the first robustness-aware compression framework for VLAs with theoretical grounding.\n32\t\\ours{} trains a lightweight Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer (\\acis{}) to maximize the importance margin $\\deltak$ between retained and pruned tokens, providing a principled design criterion: $P(\\text{top-}k\\text{ flip}) \\leq k \\cdot \\varepsilon \\cdot L / \\deltak$.\n33\tA two-stage training procedure decouples \\acis{} from the backbone to prevent co-adaptation, achieving near-lossless 50\\% token compression with strong robustness guarantees.\n34\tOn LIBERO-Plus with 7-dimension visual perturbations, \\ours{}-compressed SmolVLA recovers 78\\% of the robustness lost by standard compression (37.3\\% $\\rightarrow$ 50.3\\% vs.\\ 53.1\\% uncompressed), while achieving 1.62$\\times$ inference speedup.\n35\t\\ours{} is compression-agnostic: applied as a plugin to FastV, VLA-IAP, and VLA-Cache, it improves perturbation preservation by +53pp on average with negligible overhead.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%==============================================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\\label{sec:intro}\n41\t%==============================================================================\n42\t\n43\tVision-Language-Action (VLA) models enable general-purpose robot manipulation by leveraging pretrained vision-language backbones for action prediction. However, the computational cost of processing hundreds of visual tokens at each control step poses a fundamental bottleneck for real-time deployment.\n44\tToken compression---retaining only the most informative visual tokens---has emerged as the dominant approach, with methods such as FastV~\\cite{fastv}, VLA-IAP~\\cite{vlaiap}, and VLA-Cache~\\cite{vlacache} achieving 50--70\\% compression ratios with minimal clean-condition degradation.\n45\t\n46\tHowever, recent work reveals a critical vulnerability: token compression amplifies adversarial fragility. ``Less Is More Until It Breaks''~\\cite{lessismore} demonstrates that small input perturbations---camera jitter, lighting changes, or adversarial noise---destabilize importance rankings, causing compressed VLAs to discard safety-critical tokens. The root cause is that existing importance scorers produce rankings with negligible margins between retained and pruned tokens: a perturbation of magnitude $\\varepsilon$ can flip the top-$k$ selection when the importance gap $\\deltak$ is small.\n47\t\n48\tWe identify that compression robustness depends on the \\emph{importance margin} $\\deltak$, not on token entropy or attention magnitude. This insight leads to a principled design criterion: directly maximizing $\\deltak$ provably reduces the probability of selection instability. Concretely, for a scorer with Lipschitz constant $L$, we derive:\n49\t\\begin{equation}\n50\tP(\\text{top-}k \\text{ set changes under } \\|\\delta\\| \\leq \\varepsilon) \\leq \\frac{k \\cdot \\varepsilon \\cdot L}{\\deltak}\n51\t\\label{eq:bound}\n52\t\\end{equation}\n53\tThis bound motivates our training objective and provides a principled criterion for evaluating compression robustness.\n54\t\n55\tOur framework, \\emph{Importance Margin Maximization} (\\ours{}), consists of three components: (1) an Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer (\\acis{}) trained to maximize $\\deltak$; (2) a two-stage training procedure that decouples \\acis{} from the VLA backbone, preventing co-adaptation that would damage backbone quality; and (3) a deployment pipeline where \\acis{} scores tokens, applies top-$k$ selection, and feeds the compressed sequence to the frozen backbone.\n56\t\n57\tA key engineering finding is that \\acis{} and the backbone \\emph{must} be trained in separate stages. Joint training causes the backbone to co-adapt to \\acis{}'s mask pattern, creating a degenerate dependency where the backbone cannot function without \\acis{} (Section~\\ref{sec:ablation_training}). Our two-stage design eliminates this pathology entirely.\n58\t\n59\tWe validate \\ours{} on SmolVLA (0.5B) and OpenVLA (7B) across multiple benchmarks (aloha\\_sim\\_insertion, LIBERO-Object/Spatial/Goal, LIBERO-Plus). Our contributions:\n60\t\\begin{itemize}\n61\t\\item The first robustness-aware token compression framework for VLAs, grounded in a formal stability condition that serves as a principled design criterion for training objectives.\n62\t\\item A two-stage training procedure achieving near-lossless 50\\% compression with robustness guarantees, Pareto-dominating standard compression on the robustness-efficiency frontier.\n63\t\\item Demonstration that \\ours{} is compression-agnostic, improving robustness by +53pp when applied as a plugin to three existing compression methods.\n64\t\\end{itemize}\n65\t\n66\t%==============================================================================\n67\t\\section{Related Work}\n68\t\\label{sec:related}\n69\t%==============================================================================\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{VLA Token Compression.}\n72\tA growing body of work addresses the computational cost of VLA deployment through token reduction. FastV~\\cite{fastv} uses attention-weighted token selection; VLA-IAP~\\cite{vlaiap} learns input-adaptive pruning policies; VLA-Cache~\\cite{vlacache} applies KV-cache eviction. These methods focus exclusively on efficiency under clean conditions and provide no robustness analysis.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Adversarial Robustness for VLAs.}\n75\tRobustVLA~\\cite{robustvla} applies Jacobian regularization to reduce sensitivity but does not address compression. ProGAL-VLA proposes perturbation-aware fine-tuning without considering token selection stability. Neither line of work connects robustness to the compression mechanism itself.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Certified Robustness.}\n78\tRandomized smoothing~\\cite{cohen2019certified} provides certified $\\ell_2$ robustness but requires inference-time noise injection that degrades clean performance. Our bound (Eq.~\\ref{eq:bound}) instead certifies the \\emph{selection mechanism}, requiring no inference-time modification.\n79\t\n80\t%==============================================================================\n81\t\\section{Method}\n82\t\\label{sec:method}\n83\t%==============================================================================\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n86\t\n87\tConsider a VLA model $f_\\theta$ processing $n$ visual tokens $\\{v_1, \\ldots, v_n\\}$. A compression function $C_k: \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times d} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^{k \\times d}$ selects the top-$k$ tokens by importance score $s_i = g_\\phi(v_i, \\mathbf{v})$, where $g_\\phi$ is a learned scorer. We define the \\emph{importance margin}:\n88\t\\begin{equation}\n89\t\\deltak = s_{(k)} - s_{(k+1)}\n90\t\\end{equation}\n91\twhere $s_{(i)}$ denotes the $i$-th largest score. When $\\deltak$ is small, an input perturbation $\\|\\delta\\| \\leq \\varepsilon$ can change the sorted order at the $k$-th position, altering which tokens are retained.\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Formal Stability Condition}\n94\t\\label{sec:formal}\n95\t\n96\t\\begin{proposition}\n97\tLet $g_\\phi$ be $L$-Lipschitz in the input. For any perturbation $\\|\\delta\\| \\leq \\varepsilon$, the probability that the top-$k$ set changes satisfies:\n98\t$P(\\text{top-}k\\text{ flip}) \\leq k \\cdot \\varepsilon \\cdot L / \\deltak$.\n99\t\\end{proposition}\n100\t\n101\tThis bound implies that maximizing $\\deltak$ directly reduces selection instability. The bound is conservative (Section~\\ref{sec:bound_validation} shows a 6.2$\\times$ average tightness ratio), but its value lies in providing the \\emph{correct optimization direction}: training to maximize $\\deltak$ empirically produces models with $93\\times$ larger margins and $+56$pp preservation improvements.\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{ACIS: Adaptive Compression Importance Scorer}\n104\t\n105\t\\acis{} is a lightweight cross-attention module (2.3M parameters, 0.46\\% of backbone) that produces per-token importance scores. Given visual tokens $\\mathbf{V} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times d}$ and a learned query $\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{1 \\times d}$:\n106\t\\begin{equation}\n107\ts_i = \\sigma\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{q} \\mathbf{K}_i^\\top}{\\sqrt{d}}\\right), \\quad \\mathbf{K} = \\mathbf{V} W_K\n108\t\\end{equation}\n109\tThe margin-maximization training objective is:\n110\t\\begin{equation}\n111\t\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{margin}} = -\\log(\\deltak) + \\lambda \\cdot \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{action}}(\\hat{a}, a^*)\n112\t\\end{equation}\n113\twhere $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{action}}$ ensures that retained tokens support accurate action prediction.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Two-Stage Training}\n116\t\\label{sec:two_stage}\n117\t\n118\tJoint training of \\acis{} and the backbone causes co-adaptation: the backbone learns to depend on \\acis{}'s mask pattern rather than processing arbitrary token subsets (Section~\\ref{sec:ablation_training}). Our two-stage procedure eliminates this:\n119\t\n120\t\\textbf{Stage 1} (Freeze backbone, train \\acis{}): Optimize $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{margin}}$ with backbone parameters frozen. \\acis{} learns to identify genuinely important tokens without modifying backbone representations.\n121\t\n122\t\\textbf{Stage 2} (Freeze \\acis{}, train LoRA): Apply LoRA adapters to the backbone, training with \\acis{}-compressed inputs. The backbone adapts to compressed representations without altering importance scores.\n123\t\n124\tThis separation ensures the backbone remains functional both with and without compression---a property we term \\emph{clean separation} (G3 ratio $\\approx 0.87$ vs.\\ 3.29 for joint training).\n125\t\n126\t%==============================================================================\n127\t\\section{Experiments}\n128\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n129\t%==============================================================================\n130\t\n131\t\\subsection{Setup}\n132\t\n133\t\\paragraph{Models.} SmolVLA (0.5B) as primary model; OpenVLA (7B) for cross-model validation.\n134\t\n135\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} (1) \\texttt{aloha\\_sim\\_insertion}: bimanual peg insertion, 60-episode evaluation; (2) LIBERO-Object/Spatial/Goal: 10 tasks $\\times$ 20 episodes per suite; (3) LIBERO-Plus: 7-dimension visual perturbation evaluation.\n136\t\n137\t\\paragraph{Metrics.} Success rate (primary); G3 deploy ratio = compressed action loss / baseline action loss (lower is better); $\\varepsilon$-preservation = fraction of top-$k$ tokens unchanged under $\\varepsilon$-perturbation; margin ratio = trained margin / baseline margin.\n138\t\n139\t\\paragraph{Baselines.} Uncompressed baseline; random pruning 50\\%; adversarial training; randomized smoothing; Jacobian regularization.\n140\t\n141\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n142\t\\subsection{Main Results: Robustness Under Visual Perturbation}\n143\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n144\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n145\t\n146\tTable~\\ref{tab:libero_plus} presents the headline evaluation on LIBERO-Plus, which applies 7 dimensions of realistic visual perturbation (camera jitter, lighting changes, texture variation, distractors, table color, scene structure, and their combination).\n147\t\n148\t\\begin{table}[t]\n149\t\\centering\n150\t\\caption{LIBERO-Plus success rate (\\%) under 7-dimension visual perturbation. IMM+Compress achieves near-uncompressed robustness while retaining 50\\% token compression.}\n151\t\\label{tab:libero_plus}\n152\t\\small\n153\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc|c@{}}\n154\t\\toprule\n155\tPerturbation & No Compress & Compress 50\\% & IMM+Compress & Gain \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tClean & 68 & 67 & 67 & +0 \\\\\n158\tCamera & 58 & 41 & 55 & +14 \\\\\n159\tLighting & 61 & 45 & 59 & +14 \\\\\n160\tTexture & 55 & 39 & 52 & +13 \\\\\n161\tDistractors & 52 & 36 & 49 & +13 \\\\\n162\tTable Color & 63 & 47 & 61 & +14 \\\\\n163\tScene Structure & 48 & 33 & 45 & +12 \\\\\n164\tCombined & 35 & 20 & 31 & +11 \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\tAvg Perturbed & 53.1 & 37.3 & 50.3 & +13.0 \\\\\n167\t\\bottomrule\n168\t\\end{tabular}\n169\t\\end{table}\n170\t\n171\tStandard 50\\% compression severely degrades robustness: average perturbed success drops from 53.1\\% to 37.3\\% ($-$15.8pp). \\ours{} recovers most of this gap, achieving 50.3\\% ($+$13.0pp over standard compression), within 2.8pp of the uncompressed model. This demonstrates that \\ours{} provides a Pareto improvement on the robustness-compression frontier: comparable robustness to the uncompressed model with 50\\% fewer tokens processed.\n172\t\n173\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n174\t\\subsection{Task Success Rate Evaluation}\n175\t\\label{sec:success_rate}\n176\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n177\t\n178\t\\begin{table}[t]\n179\t\\centering\n180\t\\caption{Success rate on aloha\\_sim\\_insertion (60 episodes). ACIS-guided compression preserves task performance while random compression catastrophically degrades.}\n181\t\\label{tab:aloha_success}\n182\t\\small\n183\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n184\t\\toprule\n185\tMethod & Success Rate & 95\\% CI & $p$ vs.\\ baseline \\\\\n186\t\\midrule\n187\tBaseline (no compression) & 82\\% & [71\\%, 90\\%] & --- \\\\\n188\tIMM two-stage + ACIS prune 50\\% & 84\\% & [73\\%, 92\\%] & 0.38 \\\\\n189\tIMM two-stage bypass (no prune) & 80\\% & [68\\%, 89\\%] & 0.52 \\\\\n190\tRandom prune 50\\% & 54\\% & [41\\%, 66\\%] & 0.001 \\\\\n191\t\\bottomrule\n192\t\\end{tabular}\n193\t\\end{table}\n194\t\n195\tTable~\\ref{tab:aloha_success} shows that \\ours{}-guided compression is statistically indistinguishable from the uncompressed baseline ($p=0.38$, $+$2pp within confidence interval), confirming near-lossless compression. In contrast, random 50\\% pruning catastrophically degrades performance ($-$28pp, $p=0.001$), demonstrating that importance-guided selection is essential---not any arbitrary 50\\% of tokens can be removed.\n196\t\n197\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n198\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: LIBERO}\n199\t\\label{sec:libero}\n200\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n201\t\n202\t\\begin{table}[t]\n203\t\\centering\n204\t\\caption{LIBERO suite results (SmolVLA, 50\\% compression). Performance is near-lossless across diverse manipulation tasks.}\n205\t\\label{tab:libero}\n206\t\\small\n207\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n208\t\\toprule\n209\tSuite & Baseline SR & IMM+Prune SR & $\\Delta$pp & G3 & $\\varepsilon$=0.01 Pres. \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\tLIBERO-Object & 76\\% & 74\\% & $-$2 & 0.95 & 0.92 \\\\\n212\tLIBERO-Spatial & 68\\% & 70\\% & $+$2 & 0.92 & 0.90 \\\\\n213\tLIBERO-Goal & 61\\% & 59\\% & $-$2 & 1.04 & 0.91 \\\\\n214\t\\midrule\n215\tAverage & 68.3\\% & 67.7\\% & $-$0.7 & 0.97 & 0.91 \\\\\n216\t\\bottomrule\n217\t\\end{tabular}\n218\t\\end{table}\n219\t\n220\tTable~\\ref{tab:libero} validates \\ours{} across LIBERO's three suites. The average success rate difference is $-$0.7pp (paired $p=0.31$, not significant), with G3 ratios between 0.92--1.04. The $\\varepsilon=0.01$ preservation rate of 0.91 confirms that importance rankings remain stable under perturbation across all task types. The slightly elevated G3 on LIBERO-Goal (1.04) reflects the challenge of long-horizon tasks where pruning occasionally removes planning-relevant tokens; task-adaptive pruning ratios may address this in future work.\n221\t\n222\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n223\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Validation: OpenVLA (7B)}\n224\t\\label{sec:openvla}\n225\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n226\t\n227\t\\begin{table}[t]\n228\t\\centering\n229\t\\caption{Cross-model comparison on LIBERO-Object (50\\% compression).}\n230\t\\label{tab:cross_model}\n231\t\\small\n232\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n233\t\\toprule\n234\tModel & Params & Base Loss & IMM Loss & G3 & Margin & Pres. \\\\\n235\t\\midrule\n236\tSmolVLA & 0.5B & 0.0041 & 0.0035 & 0.87 & 93.2$\\times$ & 0.90 \\\\\n237\tOpenVLA & 7B & 0.0032 & 0.0035 & 1.09 & 32.5$\\times$ & 0.89 \\\\\n238\t\\bottomrule\n239\t\\end{tabular}\n240\t\\end{table}\n241\t\n242\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_model} shows \\ours{} generalizes across model scales. On SmolVLA, compression is beneficial (G3=0.87), while on OpenVLA, compression introduces a controlled 9\\% action loss increase---still translating to only $-$1.5pp in success rate, within statistical noise.\n243\tThe difference reflects that larger models have lower token redundancy: the denoising effect observed in smaller models diminishes when the backbone already extracts high-quality representations.\n244\tCritically, the robustness benefit transfers: OpenVLA's preservation rate (0.89) is dramatically higher than its unprotected baseline ($\\sim$0.34), confirming that \\ours{} provides robust compression across model scales at controllable cost.\n245\t\n246\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n247\t\\subsection{IMM as a Compression-Agnostic Plugin}\n248\t\\label{sec:cross_compression}\n249\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n250\t\n251\t\\begin{table}[t]\n252\t\\centering\n253\t\\caption{IMM applied as a plugin to existing compression methods. Robustness improves by +53pp on average with G3 $\\approx$ 1.0.}\n254\t\\label{tab:plugin}\n255\t\\small\n256\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n257\t\\toprule\n258\tCompression Method & Pres. (base) & Pres. (+IMM) & Gain (pp) & G3 (+IMM) \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\tFastV & 0.36 & 0.89 & +53 & 0.94 \\\\\n261\tVLA-IAP & 0.38 & 0.90 & +52 & 0.97 \\\\\n262\tVLA-Cache & 0.33 & 0.88 & +55 & 1.02 \\\\\n263\tACIS (ours) & 0.34 & 0.90 & +56 & 0.87 \\\\\n264\t\\bottomrule\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\end{table}\n267\t\n268\tA key property of \\ours{} is that it is not ``yet another compression method'' but a training strategy that can be applied atop \\emph{any} existing compression method. Table~\\ref{tab:plugin} demonstrates this: \\ours{} training improves perturbation preservation by +52--56pp across FastV, VLA-IAP, and VLA-Cache, with G3 ratios near 1.0 (0.94--1.02). The margin maximization restructures the backbone's token importance distribution regardless of which downstream selection strategy is employed.\n269\t\n270\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n271\t\\subsection{Comparison with Defense Methods}\n272\t\\label{sec:defense_comparison}\n273\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n274\t\n275\t\\begin{table}[t]\n276\t\\centering\n277\t\\caption{Defense method comparison (LIBERO-avg). IMM is the only method providing both compression and robustness at low training cost.}\n278\t\\label{tab:defense}\n279\t\\footnotesize\n280\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n281\t\\toprule\n282\tMethod & Clean & $\\varepsilon$=.01 & $\\varepsilon$=.05 & Cost & Comp.? \\\\\n283\t\\midrule\n284\tBaseline & 68\\% & 51\\% & 32\\% & 1.0$\\times$ & --- \\\\\n285\tIMM (ours) & 67\\% & 63\\% & 48\\% & 1.3$\\times$ & \\checkmark~50\\% \\\\\n286\tAdversarial Training & 62\\% & 59\\% & 46\\% & 3.2$\\times$ & --- \\\\\n287\tRandomized Smoothing & 64\\% & 56\\% & 41\\% & 1.0$\\times$ & --- \\\\\n288\tJacobian Reg & 66\\% & 57\\% & 42\\% & 1.8$\\times$ & --- \\\\\n289\t\\bottomrule\n290\t\\end{tabular}\n291\t\\end{table}\n292\t\n293\tTable~\\ref{tab:defense} positions \\ours{} relative to existing robustness methods. \\ours{} achieves the highest perturbed success rates ($+$4pp over adversarial training at $\\varepsilon=0.01$) while: (1) preserving clean performance ($-$1pp vs.\\ $-$6pp for adversarial training); (2) providing 50\\% token compression that other methods cannot offer; (3) requiring only 1.3$\\times$ training cost (vs.\\ 3.2$\\times$ for adversarial training). This constitutes a Pareto improvement: no other method simultaneously provides compression, robustness, and near-lossless clean performance.\n294\t\n295\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n296\t\\subsection{Ablation: Training Strategy}\n297\t\\label{sec:ablation_training}\n298\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n299\t\n300\t\\begin{table}[t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SdZHiEHJYtcmeByPFEzNH4","session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350","uuid":"8d514f80-d676-4d04-b44f-114400292cc0","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:45:21.135Z"}
