{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01R2uPYvTNCgXaBCL95mwa2e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NyPETUweaE8wtAGhsBJHij","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-2/projects/importance_margin_vla_compress/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T075046/median/paper.tex","offset":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12207,"cache_read_input_tokens":39570,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12207,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SdZHiEHJYtcmeByPFEzNH4","session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350","uuid":"299df54a-371f-42de-8bfe-dded6730fb6e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NyPETUweaE8wtAGhsBJHij","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t\\begin{table}[t]\n301\t\\centering\n302\t\\caption{Training strategy ablation (SmolVLA, aloha\\_sim\\_insertion). Two-stage training eliminates co-adaptation, achieving the lowest deploy G3 and highest margin.}\n303\t\\label{tab:ablation_training}\n304\t\\footnotesize\n305\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n306\t\\toprule\n307\tStrategy & Std Loss & Prune Loss & Bypass & G3 & Margin \\\\\n308\t\\midrule\n309\tJoint (v7) & 0.0134 & 0.0134 & 0.0078 & 3.29 & 0.57 \\\\\n310\tGrad-Isolated (v8) & 0.0055 & 0.0055 & 0.0289 & 1.34 & 0.50 \\\\\n311\tTwo-Stage (v8b) & 0.0207 & 0.0035 & 0.0187 & 0.87 & 0.93 \\\\\n312\tBaseline (no IMM) & 0.0041 & --- & --- & 1.00 & 0.01 \\\\\n313\t\\bottomrule\n314\t\\end{tabular}\n315\t\\end{table}\n316\t\n317\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_training} reveals why two-stage training is necessary. Joint training (v7) produces a \\emph{co-adapted} system: the backbone's bypass loss (0.0078) is far below its standard loss (0.0134), indicating the backbone has learned to depend on ACIS's mask pattern rather than processing tokens independently. The G3 ratio of 3.29 means the model is 3.3$\\times$ worse without ACIS---a dangerous deployment dependency.\n318\t\n319\tGradient isolation (v8) partially addresses this (G3=1.34) but residual co-adaptation remains. Only two-stage training (v8b) achieves clean separation: the bypass loss (0.0187) and standard loss (0.0207) are comparable, indicating the backbone functions independently of ACIS. This yields the lowest G3 (0.87) and highest margin (0.93).\n320\t\n321\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n322\t\\subsection{Ablation: Pruning Ratio}\n323\t\\label{sec:ratio_sweep}\n324\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n325\t\n326\t\\begin{table}[t]\n327\t\\centering\n328\t\\caption{Pruning ratio sweep (SmolVLA, aloha\\_sim\\_insertion). A sweet spot at 50--70\\% retention balances compression benefit and information preservation.}\n329\t\\label{tab:ratio_sweep}\n330\t\\small\n331\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n332\t\\toprule\n333\tKeep Ratio & Action Loss & G3 Ratio & $\\varepsilon$=0.01 Pres. & Tokens Kept \\\\\n334\t\\midrule\n335\t0.30 & 0.0048 & 1.18 & 0.92 & 87/290 \\\\\n336\t0.40 & 0.0042 & 1.03 & 0.91 & 116/290 \\\\\n337\t0.50 & 0.0035 & 0.87 & 0.90 & 145/290 \\\\\n338\t0.60 & 0.0034 & 0.83 & 0.89 & 174/290 \\\\\n339\t0.70 & 0.0036 & 0.89 & 0.88 & 203/290 \\\\\n340\t0.80 & 0.0039 & 0.95 & 0.87 & 232/290 \\\\\n341\t0.90 & 0.0040 & 0.99 & 0.86 & 261/290 \\\\\n342\t\\bottomrule\n343\t\\end{tabular}\n344\t\\end{table}\n345\t\n346\tTable~\\ref{tab:ratio_sweep} reveals a U-shaped G3 curve with an optimal point at 60\\% retention (G3=0.83). Below 40\\% retention, over-compression degrades quality (G3$>$1.0). Between 50--70\\%, compression is net-beneficial (G3$<$1.0), with the sweet spot at 60\\%. Above 80\\%, too few tokens are removed for any benefit. We use 50\\% as our standard operating point as it provides stronger compression while remaining in the beneficial region.\n347\t\n348\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n349\t\\subsection{Ablation: Mask Ratio Annealing}\n350\t\\label{sec:annealing}\n351\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{table}[t]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\caption{Annealing vs.\\ fixed mask ratio in Stage 1 training.}\n356\t\\label{tab:annealing}\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n359\t\\toprule\n360\tStrategy & Action Loss & G3 & Margin & $\\varepsilon$=0.01 Pres. \\\\\n361\t\\midrule\n362\tFixed 0.5 (v8b) & 0.0035 & 0.87 & 0.93 & 0.90 \\\\\n363\tAnneal 0.9$\\rightarrow$0.5 (v8c) & 0.0038 & 0.93 & 0.85 & 0.91 \\\\\n364\t\\bottomrule\n365\t\\end{tabular}\n366\t\\end{table}\n367\t\n368\tTable~\\ref{tab:annealing} compares fixed mask ratio against curriculum annealing (0.9$\\rightarrow$0.5). Annealing produces smoother training convergence but slightly worse final metrics (G3 0.93 vs.\\ 0.87, margin 0.85 vs.\\ 0.93). The small difference ($\\Delta$G3=0.06) suggests that the mask ratio is not a critical hyperparameter---results are robust within a reasonable range.\n369\t\n370\t%==============================================================================\n371\t\\section{Analysis}\n372\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n373\t%==============================================================================\n374\t\n375\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n376\t\\subsection{Formal Bound Validation}\n377\t\\label{sec:bound_validation}\n378\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n379\t\n380\t\\begin{table}[t]\n381\t\\centering\n382\t\\caption{Theoretical bound vs.\\ empirical flip rate across perturbation levels (SmolVLA, $k$=145, $L$=1.07).}\n383\t\\label{tab:bound}\n384\t\\footnotesize\n385\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n386\t\\toprule\n387\t$\\varepsilon$ & Theo.\\ $P$(flip) & Emp.\\ flip & Tight. & Holds? \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\t0.005 & 0.083 & 0.012 & 6.9$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n390\t0.010 & 0.167 & 0.025 & 6.7$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n391\t0.020 & 0.333 & 0.058 & 5.7$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n392\t0.050 & 0.834 & 0.148 & 5.6$\\times$ & \\checkmark \\\\\n393\t0.100 & 1.000 & 0.312 & --- & \\checkmark~(trivial) \\\\\n394\t\\bottomrule\n395\t\\end{tabular}\n396\t\\end{table}\n397\t\n398\tTable~\\ref{tab:bound} validates our formal stability condition. The bound is never violated (soundness holds) across all tested perturbation levels. The average tightness ratio of 6.2$\\times$ indicates the bound is conservative---expected given that it uses worst-case Lipschitz estimates over all perturbation directions. Despite looseness, the bound's value is as a \\emph{design criterion}: IMM training increases $\\deltak$ from 0.01 to 0.93 (93$\\times$), and even with a 6$\\times$ loose bound, the certified region expands by $\\sim$15$\\times$. Tightening the bound via data-dependent Lipschitz estimation is promising future work.\n399\t\n400\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n401\t\\subsection{Token Importance Analysis}\n402\t\\label{sec:token_analysis}\n403\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n404\t\n405\t\\begin{table}[t]\n406\t\\centering\n407\t\\caption{Pruned vs.\\ kept tokens. ACIS-pruned tokens have lower attention weight and gradient contribution.}\n408\t\\label{tab:token_analysis}\n409\t\\footnotesize\n410\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n411\t\\toprule\n412\tMetric & Pruned & Kept & Ratio & $d$ & $p$ \\\\\n413\t\\midrule\n414\tAvg Attention Weight & 0.0018 & 0.0041 & 0.44 & 0.42 & 0.003 \\\\\n415\tAvg Gradient Norm & 0.0012 & 0.0038 & 0.32 & 0.51 & 0.008 \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\tWe analyze whether ACIS's importance scoring aligns with task-relevant token properties (Table~\\ref{tab:token_analysis}). Pruned tokens receive significantly less attention from the action decoder (ratio 0.44, $p=0.003$) and contribute less to action loss gradients (ratio 0.32, $p=0.008$). The Spearman correlation between ACIS importance scores and attention weight is 0.38; with gradient norm, 0.45.\n421\t\n422\tThese results are consistent with an importance-guided denoising interpretation: removing low-importance tokens eliminates noise sources that would otherwise interfere with action prediction. However, the moderate effect sizes (Cohen's $d$ = 0.42--0.51) indicate distributional overlap---some pruned tokens do carry non-trivial information, and additional mechanisms (e.g., implicit regularization) likely contribute to the compression benefit. We present this as an empirical observation consistent with the denoising hypothesis rather than a causal claim.\n423\t\n424\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n425\t\\subsection{Computational Overhead}\n426\t\\label{sec:overhead}\n427\t%------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n428\t\n429\t\\begin{table}[t]\n430\t\\centering\n431\t\\caption{ACIS deployment overhead and net efficiency gain (SmolVLA, 50\\% compression).}\n432\t\\label{tab:overhead}\n433\t\\footnotesize\n434\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n435\t\\toprule\n436\tMetric & Without ACIS & With ACIS+Compress & Change \\\\\n437\t\\midrule\n438\tParameters & 500M & 502.3M & +0.46\\% \\\\\n439\tFLOPs per step & 45.2G & 27.6G & $-$39\\% (net) \\\\\n440\tLatency (ms) & 42.5 & 26.8 & $-$37\\% (net) \\\\\n441\tThroughput (tok/s) & 6800 & 11200 & +64.7\\% \\\\\n442\t\\bottomrule\n443\t\\end{tabular}\n444\t\\end{table}\n445\t\n446\tTable~\\ref{tab:overhead} demonstrates that ACIS is not a deployment burden but a deployment \\emph{accelerator}. The module adds 2.3M parameters (+0.46\\%) and 0.6ms per-step overhead, but the resulting 50\\% token compression reduces downstream FLOPs by 39\\% (net) and latency by 37\\%. The net effect is a 1.62$\\times$ inference speedup---\\ours{} simultaneously improves robustness \\emph{and} efficiency.\n447\t\n448\tTraining overhead is 1.3$\\times$ (two-stage training adds one \\acis{}-training stage), compared to 3.2$\\times$ for adversarial training and 1.8$\\times$ for Jacobian regularization.\n449\t\n450\t%==============================================================================\n451\t\\section{Discussion}\n452\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n453\t%==============================================================================\n454\t\n455\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n456\tThe formal bound (Eq.~\\ref{eq:bound}) is approximately 6$\\times$ loose on average, limiting its utility as a tight runtime certificate. Its primary value is as a training objective design criterion. On larger models (OpenVLA 7B), G3 rises to 1.09---compression introduces a small quality cost as token redundancy decreases with model capacity. Task-adaptive pruning ratios may address this.\n457\t\n458\t\\paragraph{Broader Impact.}\n459\tRobust token compression directly enables safer real-time robot deployment in uncontrolled environments. The certified stability condition provides a principled criterion for deciding whether a compressed model can be trusted under expected perturbation levels.\n460\t\n461\t%==============================================================================\n462\t\\section{Conclusion}\n463\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n464\t%==============================================================================\n465\t\n466\tWe presented Importance Margin Maximization (\\ours{}), the first robustness-aware token compression framework for VLAs with theoretical grounding. By directly maximizing the importance margin $\\deltak$, \\ours{} provides a principled design criterion that guides both the training objective and deployment decisions.\n467\tOur two-stage training eliminates ACIS-backbone co-adaptation, achieving near-lossless 50\\% compression that Pareto-dominates standard compression on the robustness-efficiency frontier.\n468\tOn LIBERO-Plus, \\ours{} recovers 78\\% of robustness lost by standard compression while providing 1.62$\\times$ inference speedup.\n469\tAs a compression-agnostic plugin, \\ours{} improves robustness of FastV, VLA-IAP, and VLA-Cache by +53pp on average.\n470\tFuture work will investigate tighter bounds via data-dependent Lipschitz estimation and task-adaptive pruning ratios for long-horizon tasks.\n471\t\n472\t%==============================================================================\n473\t% References\n474\t%==============================================================================\n475\t\\bibliographystyle{unsrtnat}\n476\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n477\t\n478\t\\bibitem{fastv}\n479\tChen, L., et al. (2024). FastV: An image is worth 1/2 tokens after layer 2. \\emph{arXiv:2403.06764}.\n480\t\n481\t\\bibitem{vlaiap}\n482\tZhang, Y., et al. (2025). VLA-IAP: Input-adaptive pruning for vision-language-action models. \\emph{ICRA 2025}.\n483\t\n484\t\\bibitem{vlacache}\n485\tWang, H., et al. (2025). VLA-Cache: Efficient KV-cache management for VLA deployment. \\emph{arXiv:2501.xxxxx}.\n486\t\n487\t\\bibitem{lessismore}\n488\tLiu, X., et al. (2025). Less is more until it breaks: Adversarial vulnerabilities of VLA token compression. \\emph{arXiv:2601.12042}.\n489\t\n490\t\\bibitem{robustvla}\n491\tPark, S., et al. (2025). RobustVLA: Jacobian regularization for robust vision-language-action models. \\emph{CoRL 2025}.\n492\t\n493\t\\bibitem{cohen2019certified}\n494\tCohen, J., et al. (2019). Certified adversarial robustness via randomized smoothing. \\emph{ICML 2019}.\n495\t\n496\t\\bibitem{cage}\n497\tLi, W., et al. (2026). CAGE: Compression-aware gradient estimation for robust VLA deployment. \\emph{ICML 2026}.\n498\t\n499\t\\bibitem{smolvla}\n500\tHuggingFace (2025). SmolVLA: A compact vision-language-action model. \\emph{Technical Report}.\n501\t\n502\t\\bibitem{openvla}\n503\tKim, M., et al. (2024). OpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model. \\emph{CoRL 2024}.\n504\t\n505\t\\bibitem{libero}\n506\tLiu, B., et al. (2024). LIBERO: Benchmarking knowledge transfer in lifelong robot learning. \\emph{NeurIPS 2024}.\n507\t\n508\t\\end{thebibliography}\n509\t\n510\t\\end{document}\n511\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SdZHiEHJYtcmeByPFEzNH4","session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350","uuid":"0f485fe7-1a93-4316-96c1-1d1a94a721eb","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:45:27.237Z"}
