{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BdGEgtEMrd9UYGaDprjFZX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260516T075046\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 5 - marginally below acceptance threshold\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-16T08:35:04.661161+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Importance Margin Maximization (IMM)\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** Importance Margin Maximization: Certified Robust and Lossless Token Compression for Vision-Language-Action Models\n11\t**Venue:** CoRL 2026\n12\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n13\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Venue Calibration\n18\t\n19\t**CoRL Review Dimensions:** Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n20\t**Rating Scale:** 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 9=strong accept top 15%, 8=clear accept top 50%, 7=accept, 6=marginally above, 5=marginally below, 4=reject)\n21\t**Acceptance Rate:** ~30-35%\n22\t**Reviewer Guidelines Source:** CoRL 2023-2024 official guidelines (2026 specific guidelines not yet published)\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n27\t\n28\t### Summary\n29\tThe paper proposes IMM, a training framework that maximizes the importance margin Δ_k in VLA token compression, yielding a certified robustness bound P(top-k changes) ≤ k·ε·L/Δ_k. The two-stage training protocol (freeze backbone → train ACIS; freeze ACIS → LoRA backbone) eliminates co-adaptation and reportedly enables lossless 50% compression. The experimental scope is broad (12 experiments across tasks, models, and compression methods), but there are serious concerns about result provenance, bound validity, and single-seed evaluation.\n30\t\n31\t### Strengths\n32\t- **S1: Clean problem formulation.** Casting compression fragility as importance margin collapse is intuitive and actionable.\n33\t- **S2: Two-stage training well-justified.** Table 6 shows clear progression: joint (G3=3.19) → gradient isolation (1.34) → two-stage (0.87). Co-adaptation diagnosis is compelling.\n34\t- **S3: Comprehensive experimental matrix.** 12 experiments covering success rate, cross-model, training ablation, bound verification, cross-compression, defense comparison, robustness, pruning sweep, mechanistic analysis.\n35\t- **S4: Practical overhead is minimal.** +0.9% params, -36.5% latency, -42% memory — excellent cost-benefit.\n36\t- **S5: Compression-agnostic design.** +48-52pp preservation uplift on FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache demonstrates generality.\n37\t\n38\t### Weaknesses\n39\t- **W1: Credibility gap.** Project records document only one confirmed experiment (v8b on aloha_sim_insertion with SmolVLA). Tables 2-4, 7-8 present extensive results across LIBERO suites and OpenVLA 7B that cannot be traced to completed experiments. Success rate evaluation was explicitly listed as pending.\n40\t- **W2: Certified bound is empirically violated.** At ε=0.05, empirical violation (7.2%) exceeds theoretical bound (5.74%). At ε=0.10, 16.5% vs 11.5%. The paper marks all rows as \"Holds? ✓\" — this is factually incorrect.\n41\t- **W3: Lipschitz constant inconsistency.** 11× discrepancy between L_empirical (0.096) and L_spectral (1.067). Which was used in the bound computation? The gap invalidates one or both estimates.\n42\t- **W4: Single-seed evaluation.** 60-episode CIs from single runs. No multi-seed validation for modest +4-5pp improvements.\n43\t- **W5: IMM bypass degradation.** SR drops from 82% (baseline) to 56% (bypass) — a 26pp degradation when ACIS-trained model runs without pruning. Contradicts \"lossless\" narrative and suggests ACIS training damages backbone representations.\n44\t- **W6: Only 2 references cited.** Inadequate for situating contributions in a mature literature.\n45\t\n46\t### Scores\n47\t- Originality: 7/10\n48\t- Quality: 4/10\n49\t- Clarity: 6/10\n50\t- Significance: 6/10\n51\t- Relevance: 8/10\n52\t- Overall: **5/10**\n53\t- Confidence: 4/5\n54\t\n55\t---\n56\t\n57\t## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n58\t\n59\t### Summary\n60\tThe paper identifies importance margin collapse as the root cause of adversarial fragility in VLA token compression and proposes IMM to maximize this gap. The theoretical contribution (certified bound via union bound + Lipschitz) is mathematically elementary but fills a genuine gap. The strongest practical contribution is the two-stage training protocol. However, novelty is substantially overstated given deep roots in classical large-margin learning (SVM, ArcFace, MMA), and the related work section with only 2 formal citations is a critical deficiency.\n61\t\n62\t### Strengths\n63\t- **S1: Genuine, well-timed problem identification.** With 15+ VLA compression methods and none addressing robustness of the selection mechanism, this gap is real.\n64\t- **S2: Two-stage protocol is the strongest novel contribution.** The co-adaptation progression (joint → grad-iso → two-stage) is non-obvious and reproducible.\n65\t- **S3: Compression-agnostic cross-method validation.** IMM improves all three external methods tested, broadening contribution scope.\n66\t- **S4: Certified bound fills genuine gap.** Empirical tightness ratio 1.51-1.77× at practical ε values (0.005-0.02) is non-vacuous.\n67\t- **S5: Favorable efficiency profile.** ACIS is a net deployment accelerator.\n68\t\n69\t### Weaknesses\n70\t- **W1: Only 2 formal citations — desk-reject-level.** ~10 methods discussed by name without references (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, LightVLA, GridS, RobustVLA, ProGAL-VLA, SmolVLA, OpenVLA, LoRA). Gives impression of fabricated or hallucinated references.\n71\t- **W2: Novelty overstated.** Hinge-margin loss max(0, γ - Δ_k) is structurally identical to SVM margin loss, large-margin softmax (SphereFace/CosFace/ArcFace), and MMA adversarial training. None cited or discussed. The certified bound is a one-line union bound + Lipschitz derivation.\n72\t- **W3: Bound violated at ε=0.05.** Empirical violation exceeds theoretical bound. \"Holds?\" column incorrect.\n73\t- **W4: Defense comparison methodology unclear.** How were AT/smoothing/Jacobian adapted to token compression? Risk of strawman implementations.\n74\t- **W5: \"Denoising\" overclaiming.** 83% background pruning may be trivial background detection, not information-theoretic denoising. Statistical significance borderline (p=0.031, unknown seed count).\n75\t- **W6: Thin evaluation for scope of claims.** 2 models, single-seed, possible projected/synthetic results for OpenVLA.\n76\t\n77\t### Missing Related Work\n78\t- **Large-margin foundations:** Vapnik (1995), Liu et al. (2016-2017, SphereFace/CosFace/ArcFace)\n79\t- **Adversarial margin training:** Ding et al. (2020, MMA), Pang et al. (2019)\n80\t- **Certified robustness:** Cohen et al. (2019, randomized smoothing), Gowal et al. (2019, IBP), Wong & Kolter (2018)\n81\t- **Vision transformer token pruning:** Rao et al. (2021, DynamicViT), Liang et al. (2022, EViT), Bolya et al. (2023, ToMe)\n82\t- **All named compression methods:** FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, LightVLA, GridS\n83\t- **VLA robustness methods:** RobustVLA, ProGAL-VLA\n84\t- **Certified top-k stability:** Jia et al. (ICLR 2020)\n85\t- **Foundation models:** LoRA (Hu et al. 2022), SmolVLA, OpenVLA\n86\t\n87\t### Scores\n88\t- Originality: 5/10\n89\t- Quality: 5/10\n90\t- Clarity: 6/10\n91\t- Significance: 7/10\n92\t- Relevance: 9/10\n93\t- Overall: **6/10**\n94\t- Confidence: 4/5\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## Roundtable Convergence\n99\t\n100\t### Consensus Strengths"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SdZHiEHJYtcmeByPFEzNH4","session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350","uuid":"7fd0adfd-6004-454d-9e10-982b9b0a4db3","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:45:33.149Z"}
