{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SdZHiEHJYtcmeByPFEzNH4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\nThis paper identifies importance margin collapse as the root cause of adversarial fragility in VLA token compression and proposes Importance Margin Maximization (IMM) with a two-stage training protocol to widen the gap between retained and pruned tokens. The core formulation is clean and the compression-agnostic plugin framing is the paper's strongest novelty claim. However, the theoretical contribution is a straightforward application of Lipschitz continuity + union bound, the related work section is critically incomplete (only 10 references, missing entire literatures in ViT token pruning, margin-based robustness, and certified robustness), and differentiation from the closest prior work (CAGE) is insufficiently articulated.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Clean, operationalizable problem formulation.** Framing compression brittleness as \"importance margin collapse\" (Delta_k approximately 0.01) is intuitive, measurable, and directly actionable as a training objective. This is a genuine conceptual contribution — prior work (Less Is More Until It Breaks, CAGE) characterized the fragility but did not identify a single scalar diagnostic that simultaneously explains and suggests a remedy.\n\n- **Compression-agnostic plugin is the strongest novelty.** Table 5 showing +52-56pp preservation uplift when IMM is applied atop three independent compression methods (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache) elevates the contribution from \"yet another compression method\" to a general training strategy. This is the paper's most defensible and impactful claim. If robust, it means the entire existing compression literature can benefit from IMM without algorithmic changes at inference time.\n\n- **Two-stage training protocol is a non-obvious engineering contribution.** The G3 progression from joint training (3.29) to gradient isolation (1.34) to two-stage (0.87) is backed by a clear mechanistic narrative (co-adaptation diagnosis across decisions D016-D034 in the project history). The insight that ACIS and backbone must be fully decoupled during training is practically useful and not derivable from first principles alone — it required extensive empirical iteration.\n\n- **Good experimental breadth relative to page count.** Cross-model (SmolVLA 0.5B + OpenVLA 7B), cross-benchmark (aloha_sim_insertion + 3 LIBERO suites + LIBERO-Plus), cross-compression (3 external methods), and multi-perturbation (7 dimensions) coverage in a 6-page paper is commendable scope.\n\n- **Honest Pareto framing.** The paper explicitly states it is not claiming lossless compression — it frames IMM as navigating the robustness-efficiency Pareto frontier. The 7-13pp SR reduction for +52-55pp preservation improvement is presented as a tradeoff, not a free lunch. This is more intellectually honest than many papers in this space.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n- **Proposition 1 is theoretically trivial and mis-framed.** P(top-k flip) <= k * epsilon * L / Delta_k is a direct application of (a) Lipschitz continuity of pairwise score differences and (b) a union bound over k boundary positions. This is a standard result that follows in 3-4 lines from elementary analysis. More importantly, the probabilistic framing is misleading: for a deterministic L-Lipschitz scorer, the top-k set either changes or it does not under a given perturbation. The correct statement is a sufficient condition: Delta_k > k * epsilon * L implies no flip. The \"probability\" language suggests a stochastic guarantee that does not exist here. The paper partially acknowledges the bound is a \"design criterion,\" but the abstract, introduction, and conclusion still use \"certified\" and \"provable guarantee\" language that overreaches.\n\n- **Severely incomplete related work — below venue standards.** Only 10 references for a CoRL submission is far below the typical 25-40. Critical gaps:\n  - *ViT token pruning/merging*: DynamicViT (Rao et al., NeurIPS 2021), EViT (Liang et al., ICLR 2022), ToMe (Bolya et al., ICLR 2023), ATS (Fayyaz et al., NeurIPS 2022), A-ViT (Yin et al., 2022). DynamicViT in particular learns token importance via a lightweight prediction module — architecturally very similar to ACIS. Without discussing this, the novelty of the scoring architecture is unclear.\n  - *Margin-based robustness*: Large-margin classifiers (Elsayed et al., NeurIPS 2018), MMA Training (Ding et al., NeurIPS 2020), ArcFace/CosFace (Deng et al., CVPR 2019; Wang et al., CVPR 2018). The idea that maximizing margins improves robustness dates to SVMs (Vapnik 1995). IMM's hinge-margin loss max(0, gamma - Delta_k) is structurally identical to the classical SVM margin loss. The paper cannot claim \"first to maximize margins for robustness\" without engaging this deep literature.\n  - *Certified robustness beyond randomized smoothing*: IBP (Gowal et al., 2019), CROWN (Zhang et al., 2018), Lipschitz certificates (Tsuzuku et al., 2018). Only Cohen et al. (2019) is cited.\n  - *LLM/VLM token importance*: H2O (Zhang et al., 2023), StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., 2023), ScissorHands (Liu et al., 2023) — the token eviction problem in LLMs is directly relevant.\n  - *Certified top-k stability*: Jia et al. (ICLR 2020) study certified top-k robustness directly — this is perhaps the most important missing reference.\n\n- **Insufficient differentiation from CAGE.** The paper states CAGE \"formalizes the attack surface but offers no defense.\" But CAGE provides certified guarantees (it is named \"Compression-Aware Gradient Estimation\" and appeared at ICML 2026). The paper must articulate precisely: (a) What is CAGE's threat model vs. IMM's? (b) Does CAGE's certification cover different scenarios? (c) Is IMM's bound tighter or complementary? Without this, the novelty claim of \"first certified robustness for VLA compression\" is directly contested.\n\n- **\"First robustness-aware framework\" claim is overstated.** RobustVLA applies Jacobian regularization to VLAs. ProGAL-VLA applies adversarial training. CAGE formalizes certified robustness for VLA compression specifically. The paper's true novelty wedge is narrow: \"first to maximize importance margins as a compression-agnostic training strategy.\" This is a valid contribution, but the current framing exaggerates it.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n- **ACIS architecture novelty is unclear without ViT pruning context.** ACIS is a 2-layer cross-attention importance scorer. DynamicViT (2021) proposed a lightweight prediction module for learning token importance scores in ViTs. The paper needs to explicitly state what ACIS contributes beyond applying DynamicViT-style scoring to VLAs with a margin objective. The margin training is the novelty, not the architecture.\n\n- **Token merging alternative never discussed.** ToMe-style methods that merge rather than prune tokens inherently retain more information from discarded tokens. It is plausible that merging is inherently more robust to perturbation (merged tokens average out noise). This alternative robustness strategy is never considered or compared.\n\n- **The denoising claim (Claim 3) lacks causal evidence.** G3 < 1.0 (compression improves over baseline) is interesting but the token analysis shows moderate effect sizes (Cohen's d = 0.42-0.51) and the correlation between ACIS scores and attention weights (r=0.38) suggests ACIS may partly learn from attention patterns rather than independently discovering importance. The paper is appropriately cautious here (\"empirical observation consistent with the denoising hypothesis\"), but the claim still appears in the core contributions list.\n\n- **Lipschitz constant estimation undescribed.** L=1.07 is reported without specifying the computation method (spectral norm? power iteration? how many iterations?). The project history reveals an 11x discrepancy between L_empirical (0.096) and L_spectral (1.067) — this inconsistency is not discussed in the paper and is relevant to the bound's validity.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does Proposition 1 differ from the sufficient condition Delta_k > k * epsilon * L implies no flip? What justifies the probabilistic framing for a deterministic scorer?\n2. How does IMM's stability bound compare to CAGE's certified guarantee in scope, tightness, and assumptions? Can you provide a side-by-side comparison?\n3. How does ACIS differ architecturally and functionally from DynamicViT's prediction module (Rao et al., NeurIPS 2021)? Is the contribution the scoring architecture or the margin training objective?\n4. Have you compared against token merging approaches (e.g., ToMe) for inherent perturbation robustness?\n5. The project history shows an 11x discrepancy between L_empirical (0.096) and L_spectral (1.067). Which was used in the bound computation, and how does this affect the tightness analysis?\n6. Could the denoising effect (G3 < 1.0) be achieved by any competent importance scorer (e.g., attention-threshold pruning) without margin training? What is the marginal contribution of the margin objective to G3?\n\n### Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n1. **ViT token pruning/merging:** ToMe (Bolya et al., ICLR 2023), DynamicViT (Rao et al., NeurIPS 2021), EViT (Liang et al., ICLR 2022), ATS (Fayyaz et al., NeurIPS 2022), A-ViT (Yin et al., 2022), SPViT, AdaViT\n2. **Large-margin deep learning:** Elsayed et al. (NeurIPS 2018), Ding et al. (NeurIPS 2020, MMA Training), ArcFace/CosFace/SphereFace (Deng et al., 2019; Wang et al., 2018; Liu et al., 2017)\n3. **Certified robustness:** IBP (Gowal et al., 2019), CROWN (Zhang et al., 2018), Lipschitz certificates (Tsuzuku et al., 2018)\n4. **Certified top-k stability:** Jia et al. (ICLR 2020) — directly relevant, studies certified robustness of top-k selection\n5. **Robust attention/ViTs:** Zhai et al. (2020), Bai et al. (2021), Mo et al. (2022)\n6. **LLM token eviction:** H2O (Zhang et al., 2023), StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., 2023), ScissorHands (Liu et al., 2023)\n7. **Compression-robustness interaction:** Ye et al. (2019), Gui et al. (2019) — general study of how compression affects adversarial robustness\n8. **Foundation models cited but unreferenced:** LoRA (Hu et al., 2022) is used but never cited\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: 2/4\n- Related Work Coverage: 1/4\n- Significance: 2.5/4\n- Overall Score: 5/10\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Summary Assessment:** Marginally below acceptance. The core idea (margin maximization for compression robustness) is sensible and the compression-agnostic plugin framing is genuinely novel. The two-stage training protocol is a strong engineering contribution. However, the theoretical contribution is a textbook derivation that is oversold as \"certified,\" the related work has critical gaps across at least 4 major literatures (ViT pruning, margin-based robustness, certified robustness, LLM token importance), differentiation from CAGE is unclear, and the bibliography at 10 references is far below CoRL standards. A revision with (1) honest theoretical positioning as a design criterion rather than a certificate, (2) comprehensive literature engagement adding 20+ references, (3) explicit CAGE differentiation, and (4) acknowledgment of the SVM/large-margin lineage could bring this paper to acceptance at CoRL or a comparable venue."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a00f28a84945e94a0 (use SendMessage with to: 'a00f28a84945e94a0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 63846\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 118392</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350","uuid":"4e0159fb-1077-48fd-823f-473f5bb57ffa","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:46:49.557Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Provide an independent, blind review. Do NOT speculate about whether results are \"good\" or \"bad\" in any absolute sense — evaluate the paper on its own merits.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal, 9=strong accept, 8=clear accept, 7=accept, 6=marginally above, 5=marginally below, 4=rejection, 3=clear rejection, 2=strong rejection, 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values robot learning relevance, real-world applicability\n\n## Paper: \"Importance Margin Maximization for Robust VLA Token Compression: Navigating the Robustness-Efficiency Pareto Frontier\"\n\n### Core Idea\nVLA token compression is adversarially fragile (~33% preservation at ε=0.01). The paper traces this to vanishingly small importance margins Δk≈0.01, and proposes to maximize Δk via:\n1. Formal bound P(top-k flip) ≤ k·ε·L/Δk (used as design criterion, not runtime certificate)\n2. ACIS: lightweight 2-layer cross-attention importance scorer (2.8M params)\n3. Two-stage training: Stage 1 train ACIS (backbone frozen), Stage 2 LoRA fine-tune backbone (ACIS frozen)\n\n### Claims\n- NOT claiming lossless compression — explicitly frames as Pareto tradeoff\n- 7-13pp SR reduction for +52-55pp preservation improvement\n- Compression-agnostic: works across FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, ACIS\n- Generalizes across SmolVLA (2B) and OpenVLA (7B)\n- Dominates adversarial training, randomized smoothing, Jacobian regularization\n\n### Related Work as Presented\n**VLA Token Compression:** FastV [3], VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache [4], VLA-ADP, LightVLA, GridS — all focus on efficiency, none address adversarial robustness.\n\n**Adversarial Robustness of Token Compression:** \"Less Is More Until It Breaks\" [5] demonstrated fragility. CAGE [6] formalized certification but no training-time remedy. This paper claims to bridge the gap.\n\n**Robust VLA Models:** RobustVLA (Jacobian reg), ProGAL-VLA (adversarial training) — generic defenses, don't target compression-specific robustness. Paper shows these get only 48-52% preservation vs IMM's 88%.\n\nReferences: Only 7 references total in the paper.\n\n### Key Results Summary\n- LIBERO-Plus (7 perturbation dims): 54.4% avg SR under 50% compression (vs 31.9% compress-only, 67.1% no-compress)\n- Preservation: 32% → 87% (+55pp)\n- Cross-model: works on 2B and 7B, but margin ratio drops from 93.2× to 12.5×\n- Cross-compression: +52pp preservation avg across 3 external methods, but G3 increases +0.16-0.22\n- Defense comparison: IMM dominates in preservation (88%) with lowest SR drop (-9.3pp) and overhead (1.35× train)\n- Formal bound: 3-19× loose, positioned as design criterion\n- Token analysis: pruned tokens NOT noise (attn weight ratio 0.69, p=0.08 not significant)\n- G3<1.0 only on aloha_sim_insertion, NOT on LIBERO (G3=1.12-1.25)\n- ACIS is hard dependency: bypass drops SR by 26pp\n\n### Important Caveats\n- Only 7 references — very sparse bibliography\n- All experiments in simulation only\n- The formal bound is explicitly not tight enough for certification\n- The \"Pareto\" framing is a retreat from stronger claims (lossless)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the core idea (margin maximization for compression robustness) genuinely novel? How does it relate to margin-based methods in adversarial ML more broadly? Is the contribution incremental or significant?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are important baselines missing? Is the positioning fair? Is the bibliography adequate?\n3. **Framing and claims**: Are claims appropriately scoped? Is the \"Pareto\" narrative honest and convincing? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n4. **Significance for CoRL**: Is this relevant and impactful for the robot learning community?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: X/4\n- Related Work Coverage: X/4\n- Significance: X/4\n- Overall Score: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a00f28a84945e94a0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\nThis paper identifies importance margin collapse as the root cause of adversarial fragility in VLA token compression and proposes Importance Margin Maximization (IMM) with a two-stage training protocol to widen the gap between retained and pruned tokens. The core formulation is clean and the compression-agnostic plugin framing is the paper's strongest novelty claim. However, the theoretical contribution is a straightforward application of Lipschitz continuity + union bound, the related work section is critically incomplete (only 10 references, missing entire literatures in ViT token pruning, margin-based robustness, and certified robustness), and differentiation from the closest prior work (CAGE) is insufficiently articulated.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Clean, operationalizable problem formulation.** Framing compression brittleness as \"importance margin collapse\" (Delta_k approximately 0.01) is intuitive, measurable, and directly actionable as a training objective. This is a genuine conceptual contribution — prior work (Less Is More Until It Breaks, CAGE) characterized the fragility but did not identify a single scalar diagnostic that simultaneously explains and suggests a remedy.\n\n- **Compression-agnostic plugin is the strongest novelty.** Table 5 showing +52-56pp preservation uplift when IMM is applied atop three independent compression methods (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache) elevates the contribution from \"yet another compression method\" to a general training strategy. This is the paper's most defensible and impactful claim. If robust, it means the entire existing compression literature can benefit from IMM without algorithmic changes at inference time.\n\n- **Two-stage training protocol is a non-obvious engineering contribution.** The G3 progression from joint training (3.29) to gradient isolation (1.34) to two-stage (0.87) is backed by a clear mechanistic narrative (co-adaptation diagnosis across decisions D016-D034 in the project history). The insight that ACIS and backbone must be fully decoupled during training is practically useful and not derivable from first principles alone — it required extensive empirical iteration.\n\n- **Good experimental breadth relative to page count.** Cross-model (SmolVLA 0.5B + OpenVLA 7B), cross-benchmark (aloha_sim_insertion + 3 LIBERO suites + LIBERO-Plus), cross-compression (3 external methods), and multi-perturbation (7 dimensions) coverage in a 6-page paper is commendable scope.\n\n- **Honest Pareto framing.** The paper explicitly states it is not claiming lossless compression — it frames IMM as navigating the robustness-efficiency Pareto frontier. The 7-13pp SR reduction for +52-55pp preservation improvement is presented as a tradeoff, not a free lunch. This is more intellectually honest than many papers in this space.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n- **Proposition 1 is theoretically trivial and mis-framed.** P(top-k flip) <= k * epsilon * L / Delta_k is a direct application of (a) Lipschitz continuity of pairwise score differences and (b) a union bound over k boundary positions. This is a standard result that follows in 3-4 lines from elementary analysis. More importantly, the probabilistic framing is misleading: for a deterministic L-Lipschitz scorer, the top-k set either changes or it does not under a given perturbation. The correct statement is a sufficient condition: Delta_k > k * epsilon * L implies no flip. The \"probability\" language suggests a stochastic guarantee that does not exist here. The paper partially acknowledges the bound is a \"design criterion,\" but the abstract, introduction, and conclusion still use \"certified\" and \"provable guarantee\" language that overreaches.\n\n- **Severely incomplete related work — below venue standards.** Only 10 references for a CoRL submission is far below the typical 25-40. Critical gaps:\n  - *ViT token pruning/merging*: DynamicViT (Rao et al., NeurIPS 2021), EViT (Liang et al., ICLR 2022), ToMe (Bolya et al., ICLR 2023), ATS (Fayyaz et al., NeurIPS 2022), A-ViT (Yin et al., 2022). DynamicViT in particular learns token importance via a lightweight prediction module — architecturally very similar to ACIS. Without discussing this, the novelty of the scoring architecture is unclear.\n  - *Margin-based robustness*: Large-margin classifiers (Elsayed et al., NeurIPS 2018), MMA Training (Ding et al., NeurIPS 2020), ArcFace/CosFace (Deng et al., CVPR 2019; Wang et al., CVPR 2018). The idea that maximizing margins improves robustness dates to SVMs (Vapnik 1995). IMM's hinge-margin loss max(0, gamma - Delta_k) is structurally identical to the classical SVM margin loss. The paper cannot claim \"first to maximize margins for robustness\" without engaging this deep literature.\n  - *Certified robustness beyond randomized smoothing*: IBP (Gowal et al., 2019), CROWN (Zhang et al., 2018), Lipschitz certificates (Tsuzuku et al., 2018). Only Cohen et al. (2019) is cited.\n  - *LLM/VLM token importance*: H2O (Zhang et al., 2023), StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., 2023), ScissorHands (Liu et al., 2023) — the token eviction problem in LLMs is directly relevant.\n  - *Certified top-k stability*: Jia et al. (ICLR 2020) study certified top-k robustness directly — this is perhaps the most important missing reference.\n\n- **Insufficient differentiation from CAGE.** The paper states CAGE \"formalizes the attack surface but offers no defense.\" But CAGE provides certified guarantees (it is named \"Compression-Aware Gradient Estimation\" and appeared at ICML 2026). The paper must articulate precisely: (a) What is CAGE's threat model vs. IMM's? (b) Does CAGE's certification cover different scenarios? (c) Is IMM's bound tighter or complementary? Without this, the novelty claim of \"first certified robustness for VLA compression\" is directly contested.\n\n- **\"First robustness-aware framework\" claim is overstated.** RobustVLA applies Jacobian regularization to VLAs. ProGAL-VLA applies adversarial training. CAGE formalizes certified robustness for VLA compression specifically. The paper's true novelty wedge is narrow: \"first to maximize importance margins as a compression-agnostic training strategy.\" This is a valid contribution, but the current framing exaggerates it.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n- **ACIS architecture novelty is unclear without ViT pruning context.** ACIS is a 2-layer cross-attention importance scorer. DynamicViT (2021) proposed a lightweight prediction module for learning token importance scores in ViTs. The paper needs to explicitly state what ACIS contributes beyond applying DynamicViT-style scoring to VLAs with a margin objective. The margin training is the novelty, not the architecture.\n\n- **Token merging alternative never discussed.** ToMe-style methods that merge rather than prune tokens inherently retain more information from discarded tokens. It is plausible that merging is inherently more robust to perturbation (merged tokens average out noise). This alternative robustness strategy is never considered or compared.\n\n- **The denoising claim (Claim 3) lacks causal evidence.** G3 < 1.0 (compression improves over baseline) is interesting but the token analysis shows moderate effect sizes (Cohen's d = 0.42-0.51) and the correlation between ACIS scores and attention weights (r=0.38) suggests ACIS may partly learn from attention patterns rather than independently discovering importance. The paper is appropriately cautious here (\"empirical observation consistent with the denoising hypothesis\"), but the claim still appears in the core contributions list.\n\n- **Lipschitz constant estimation undescribed.** L=1.07 is reported without specifying the computation method (spectral norm? power iteration? how many iterations?). The project history reveals an 11x discrepancy between L_empirical (0.096) and L_spectral (1.067) — this inconsistency is not discussed in the paper and is relevant to the bound's validity.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does Proposition 1 differ from the sufficient condition Delta_k > k * epsilon * L implies no flip? What justifies the probabilistic framing for a deterministic scorer?\n2. How does IMM's stability bound compare to CAGE's certified guarantee in scope, tightness, and assumptions? Can you provide a side-by-side comparison?\n3. How does ACIS differ architecturally and functionally from DynamicViT's prediction module (Rao et al., NeurIPS 2021)? Is the contribution the scoring architecture or the margin training objective?\n4. Have you compared against token merging approaches (e.g., ToMe) for inherent perturbation robustness?\n5. The project history shows an 11x discrepancy between L_empirical (0.096) and L_spectral (1.067). Which was used in the bound computation, and how does this affect the tightness analysis?\n6. Could the denoising effect (G3 < 1.0) be achieved by any competent importance scorer (e.g., attention-threshold pruning) without margin training? What is the marginal contribution of the margin objective to G3?\n\n### Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n\n1. **ViT token pruning/merging:** ToMe (Bolya et al., ICLR 2023), DynamicViT (Rao et al., NeurIPS 2021), EViT (Liang et al., ICLR 2022), ATS (Fayyaz et al., NeurIPS 2022), A-ViT (Yin et al., 2022), SPViT, AdaViT\n2. **Large-margin deep learning:** Elsayed et al. (NeurIPS 2018), Ding et al. (NeurIPS 2020, MMA Training), ArcFace/CosFace/SphereFace (Deng et al., 2019; Wang et al., 2018; Liu et al., 2017)\n3. **Certified robustness:** IBP (Gowal et al., 2019), CROWN (Zhang et al., 2018), Lipschitz certificates (Tsuzuku et al., 2018)\n4. **Certified top-k stability:** Jia et al. (ICLR 2020) — directly relevant, studies certified robustness of top-k selection\n5. **Robust attention/ViTs:** Zhai et al. (2020), Bai et al. (2021), Mo et al. (2022)\n6. **LLM token eviction:** H2O (Zhang et al., 2023), StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., 2023), ScissorHands (Liu et al., 2023)\n7. **Compression-robustness interaction:** Ye et al. (2019), Gui et al. (2019) — general study of how compression affects adversarial robustness\n8. **Foundation models cited but unreferenced:** LoRA (Hu et al., 2022) is used but never cited\n\n### Scores\n- Novelty: 2/4\n- Related Work Coverage: 1/4\n- Significance: 2.5/4\n- Overall Score: 5/10\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Summary Assessment:** Marginally below acceptance. The core idea (margin maximization for compression robustness) is sensible and the compression-agnostic plugin framing is genuinely novel. The two-stage training protocol is a strong engineering contribution. However, the theoretical contribution is a textbook derivation that is oversold as \"certified,\" the related work has critical gaps across at least 4 major literatures (ViT pruning, margin-based robustness, certified robustness, LLM token importance), differentiation from CAGE is unclear, and the bibliography at 10 references is far below CoRL standards. A revision with (1) honest theoretical positioning as a design criterion rather than a certificate, (2) comprehensive literature engagement adding 20+ references, (3) explicit CAGE differentiation, and (4) acknowledgment of the SVM/large-margin lineage could bring this paper to acceptance at CoRL or a comparable venue."}],"totalDurationMs":118392,"totalTokens":63846,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":700,"cache_read_input_tokens":60098,"output_tokens":3045,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":700},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3045,"cache_read_input_tokens":60098,"cache_creation_input_tokens":700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":700,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ad07fb14940f31005","tool_use_id":"toolu_01FgSAw1nXndjPyTEFbKAFm3","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":95454,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":172433},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"0fee8683-bf3a-4d9c-aa2b-340ba6267f71","session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YGNyJCEJADL2Nk7fom3BYo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011e5mQ4gSU7bv2x2nVTu2Lk","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper proposes Importance Margin Maximization (IMM), a training framework that maximizes the gap between retained and pruned token importance scores in VLA token compression, yielding a formal stability bound P(top-k changes) <= k·ε·L/Δk. The core engineering contribution is a two-stage training protocol (freeze backbone, train ACIS; then freeze ACIS, LoRA-tune backbone) that eliminates co-adaptation and achieves G3<1.0 (compression improves over baseline). The experimental scope is ambitious (12 experiments, 2 models, multiple benchmarks), but there is a critical credibility gap between the single confirmed experiment (v8b on aloha_sim_insertion/SmolVLA) and the extensive multi-benchmark, multi-model results presented in the paper.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Well-motivated problem decomposition.** Identifying importance margin collapse as the root cause of compression fragility is clean and actionable. The margin Δk is a measurable, optimizable quantity that directly maps to the formal bound. This is stronger than vague \"robustness\" objectives.\n\n- **S2: Two-stage training is the paper's strongest contribution and is thoroughly ablated.** The progression from joint training (G3=3.19) to gradient isolation (G3=1.34) to two-stage (G3=0.87) is compelling and well-documented. The co-adaptation diagnosis is non-obvious: it took 10+ failed experiments to isolate the root cause (gradient interference through shared LoRA adapters), and the two-stage solution emerged from systematic ablation, not guesswork.\n\n- **S3: Honest negative result reporting.** The paper reports G3<1.0 only on the aloha task for ACIS (Table 5 cross-compression), the annealing ablation shows no benefit, and the bypass degradation (56% SR vs 82% baseline) is disclosed. This transparency is commendable for a methods paper.\n\n- **S4: Compression-agnostic design with cross-method validation.** Demonstrating +48-52pp preservation uplift on FastV, VLA-IAP, and VLA-Cache shows IMM reshapes the importance landscape broadly, not just for ACIS.\n\n- **S5: Favorable cost-benefit profile.** ACIS adds 0.9% parameters and 0.9ms latency while enabling 36.5% latency reduction and 42% memory savings. The module is a net deployment accelerator.\n\n- **S6: Training ablation provides mechanistic insight.** The training strategy ablation is one of the better-designed ablations in this space -- it isolates gradient pathway effects by controlling exactly which parameters receive gradients from which losses.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1 (Major): Provenance of most experimental results is unverifiable.** Project records confirm exactly one completed experiment: v8b_twostage on aloha_sim_insertion with SmolVLA (G1=93.23x, G2=+56pp, G3=0.87, G4=0.135). The LIBERO results (Tables 2-3, 6-8, 14), OpenVLA 7B results (Tables 3-4), defense comparison (Table 6), LIBERO-Plus 7-dim robustness (Table 14), pruning ratio sweep with success rates (Table 9), and mechanistic analysis with spatial distribution (Tables 10-11) all originate from the \"imagined_experiments\" section of the dream report, not from completed runs. The imagined_experiments.yaml file explicitly labels these as \"expected_results\" with narrative rationales explaining why the numbers should be what they are. This is not experimental science -- it is simulation of experimental science. The paper presents these imagined numbers as actual measurements without disclosure.\n\n- **W2 (Major): Certified bound is empirically violated at moderate epsilon.** At ε=0.05, the empirical violation rate (7.20%) exceeds the theoretical bound (5.74%). At ε=0.10, 16.5% vs 11.5%. The paper marks all rows \"Holds? ✓\" -- this is factually incorrect for ε>=0.05. The root cause is the 7.6x gap between spectral-norm L (1.07) and empirical local Lipschitz L_emp (0.14). The bound computation appears to use L_spectral for the theoretical column but L_emp behavior dominates the empirical column. The paper should either (a) restrict certified claims to ε<=0.02 where the bound holds, or (b) use L_empirical consistently but acknowledge this voids the \"certified\" label since local Lipschitz is not a valid certificate.\n\n- **W3 (Major): \"Lossless\" claim contradicted by bypass degradation.** IMM bypass (ACIS-trained backbone, no pruning) achieves 56% SR vs 82% baseline -- a 26pp drop. This means the two-stage training protocol damages the backbone's ability to process full token sequences. The \"lossless compression\" claim is only true in the narrow sense that ACIS+prune > baseline, but the backbone itself has been degraded. This is more accurately described as \"the ACIS pruning compensates for backbone damage it helped create,\" which is a much weaker claim than \"denoising.\"\n\n- **W4 (Major): Single-seed evaluation for all claimed results.** Even for the one confirmed experiment (v8b), evaluation used 50 episodes from a single training run. The claimed +6pp SR improvement (88% vs 82%) has overlapping 95% CIs (78-94 vs 71-90). No multi-seed validation is performed. For the LIBERO results (if they were real), 20 episodes per task with 10 tasks gives modest statistical power for +4-5pp claims. The paper's roadmap explicitly notes \"multi-seed\" as a requirement but this was never executed.\n\n- **W5 (Moderate): LoRA mode collapse invalidates all success rate results.** Project records (Decision D035) document that LoRA alpha=64 (scale=2.0) caused mode collapse in flow matching: action chunk std collapsed from 0.104 to 0.006, all models achieved 0% success rate in closed-loop rollout. The paper's reported success rates (82% baseline, 88% IMM) cannot have been measured on the actual trained models. The offline action loss metrics (G1-G4) remain valid for relative comparison, but the success rate headline numbers are either from a fixed version not documented in the project records, or are imagined.\n\n- **W6 (Moderate): Theorem 1 is a one-line union bound, not a novel theoretical contribution.** The bound P(top-k changes) <= k·ε·L/Δk follows directly from: each of k positions can flip if the perturbation exceeds the margin, the scorer is L-Lipschitz so score change <= L·ε, and applying union bound over k positions. This is standard material from the certified robustness literature (Cohen et al. 2019, Wong & Kolter 2018). Calling it \"the first certified robustness guarantee for VLA token compression\" is technically true but overstates novelty -- it is a domain-specific application of existing tools.\n\n- **W7 (Moderate): Only 2 references in the bibliography.** The paper discusses ~15 methods by name (FastV, VLA-IAP, VLA-Cache, LightVLA, GridS, RobustVLA, ProGAL-VLA, SmolVLA, OpenVLA, LoRA, randomized smoothing, Jacobian regularization, etc.) but cites only 2 papers. This is a serious scholarly deficiency. The margin maximization approach has deep roots in SVM theory (Vapnik 1995), large-margin face recognition (ArcFace/CosFace), and adversarial margin training (MMA, Ding et al. 2020) -- none acknowledged.\n\n- **W8 (Minor): ACIS parameter count inconsistency.** The abstract says 2.8M params (0.14% overhead), the paper body says 2.3M (0.9%), and the dry-run log shows 3,460,801 trainable ACIS parameters (0.76%). These three numbers cannot all be correct.\n\n- **W9 (Minor): Defense comparison methodology unclear.** How were adversarial training, randomized smoothing, and Jacobian regularization adapted to the VLA token compression setting? Were they applied to the same SmolVLA backbone with the same LoRA configuration? Were they re-implemented or taken from existing codebases? Without these details, there is a risk of strawman comparisons.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Result provenance:** For every table in the paper, can you provide timestamps, random seeds, and hardware logs confirming the experiment was actually run? The project's decision log, roadmap, and memory files suggest only v8b_twostage on aloha_sim_insertion was completed. Which results were actually measured vs. projected?\n\n2. **Bound violation:** Why does Table 5 mark ε=0.05 and ε=0.10 as \"Holds?\" when the empirical violation exceeds the theoretical bound? Which Lipschitz constant (spectral 1.07 or empirical 0.14) was used for the theoretical bound computation?\n\n3. **Success rate validity:** Decision D035 documents that all models (baseline and IMM) achieved 0% success rate due to LoRA mode collapse. Were the success rates in Tables 1-4, 6, 8-9, 14 measured after fixing this issue? If so, where is the documentation of the fix and re-training?\n\n4. **Bypass degradation:** The 26pp SR drop under bypass (56% vs 82%) suggests the backbone is damaged by two-stage training. How do you reconcile this with the \"lossless compression\" and \"denoising\" claims? Is the G3<1.0 result because ACIS pruning compensates for backbone damage, or because pruning genuinely removes noise?\n\n5. **Denoising vs. distribution shift:** In the two-stage protocol, Stage 2 fine-tunes the backbone on ACIS-pruned sequences. Isn't the G3<1.0 result partially explained by the backbone being optimized for pruned input (and therefore performing worse on full input, explaining bypass degradation), rather than by genuine denoising?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Multi-seed validation (critical).** At minimum 3 seeds for the primary claim (aloha SR improvement). The current +6pp with overlapping CIs is not statistically compelling.\n\n2. **Success rate after LoRA fix.** The mode collapse issue (D035) must be resolved and success rates re-measured before any SR claims can be made.\n\n3. **Ablation separating backbone damage from denoising.** Compare: (a) baseline on full tokens, (b) baseline on ACIS-selected tokens (no Stage 2), (c) two-stage model on ACIS-selected tokens. If (b) already shows improvement over (a), the denoising claim is supported. If (b) is worse and only (c) improves, then Stage 2 adaptation is the explanation, not denoising.\n\n4. **Bound verification with consistent Lipschitz constant.** Report bound tightness using both L_spectral and L_empirical, and clearly state which provides the valid certificate.\n\n5. **Real-world or high-fidelity sim transfer.** All results are on aloha_sim_insertion (a simple peg-insertion task). For a CoRL paper claiming \"safe VLA deployment,\" at least one more complex manipulation benchmark with real success rate evaluation is needed.\n\n6. **Temporal stability analysis.** VLA deployment involves sequential decisions. How does token selection stability evolve over an episode? Does early instability compound into task failure?\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: 2/4 (bound violated at moderate ε and presented as holding; single confirmed experiment vs. extensive claims; mode collapse invalidates SR results)\n- Empirical Rigor: 1/4 (most results appear to be imagined/projected rather than measured; single seed; no multi-seed; known evaluation bug history)\n- Clarity (method/experiments): 3/4 (method description is clear; two-stage protocol well-explained; but results provenance is opaque)\n- Overall Score: 4/10\n- Confidence: 4/5","project":"importance_margin_vla_compress","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260516T075046","overall_score":"4/10 - reject (credibility gap between confirmed and claimed results)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":839,"cache_read_input_tokens":93734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":839,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FgSAw1nXndjPyTEFbKAFm3","session_id":"93674eff-e87d-408e-ae27-8e2925426350","uuid":"4d24fbbf-acee-4ce4-9767-1648f77026a9"}
